{"abstract":"In this paper, we give a conditional lower bound of <i>n</i><sup>ω(<i>k</i>)</sup> on running time for the classic <i>k</i>-median and <i>k</i>-means clustering objectives (where <i>n</i> is the size of the input), even in low-dimensional Euclidean space of dimension four, assuming the Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH). We also consider <i>k</i>-median (and <i>k</i>-means) with penalties where each point need not be assigned to a center, in which case it must pay a penalty, and extend our lower bound to at least three-dimensional Euclidean space. This stands in stark contrast to many other geometric problems such as the traveling salesman problem, or computing an independent set of unit spheres. While these problems benefit from the so-called (limited) blessing of dimensionality, as they can be solved in time <i>n</i><sup><i>O</i></sup>(<i>k</i><sup>1--1/<i>d</i>)</sup> or 2<sup><i>n</i></sup><sup>1--1/<i>d</i></sup> in <i>d</i> dimensions, our work shows that widely-used clustering objectives have a lower bound of <i>n</i><sup>ω(<i>k</i>)</sup>, even in dimension four. We complete the picture by considering the two-dimensional case: we show that there is no algorithm that solves the penalized version in time less than [Equation], and provide a matching upper bound of [Equation]. The main tool we use to establish these lower bounds is the placement of points on the moment curve, which takes its inspiration from constructions of point sets yielding Delaunay complexes of high complexity.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Cohen-Addad, Vincent","de Mesmay, Arnaud","Rotenberg, Eva","Roytman, Alan"],"cuid":["S02-096.22-034489"],"event":{"acronym":"SODA","end":"2018-01-10","place":"New Orleans, us","start":"2018-01-07","title":"29th Annual ACM-SIAM Symposium on Discrete Algorithms, SODA 2018","year":"2018"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["France"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Centre National de la Recherche Scientifique (CNRS)","Sorbonne Université","University of Grenoble Alpes","Université Grenoble Alpes"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the bane of low dimensionality clustering","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/142465900/1711.01171.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S02-096.22-034489","identifier":[{"id":"85045560148","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-54394c71-62bd-423e-9e53-a3e2f259317d","ku-c4d1ffa0-a86c-497e-bb0b-e96d63653f99"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-896AB2345C7AF5AA7DC470E7F8D177DC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/142465900/1711.01171.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Department of Computer Science","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, The Image Section"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"fr","key":"centre national de la recherche scientifique","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Centre National de la Recherche Scientifique (CNRS)","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"fr","key":"université grenoble alpes","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Université Grenoble Alpes","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"fr","key":"university of grenoble alpes","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Grenoble Alpes","used":1},{"affno":"5","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science, Algorithms and Logic"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"fr","key":"sorbonne université","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Sorbonne Université","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","first":"Vincent","key":"cohenaddadpau","last":"Cohen-Addad","locid":"ca0fa5c1-07c4-4462-bbad-99abae20b107","name":"Cohen-Addad, Vincent","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Arnaud","key":"demesmaypau","last":"de Mesmay","name":"de Mesmay, Arnaud","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5 1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Eva","key":"rotenbergpau","last":"Rotenberg","locid":"1a970eb9-57c6-4f5f-a453-117cbc7705ba","name":"Rotenberg, Eva","orcid":"0000-0001-5853-7909","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 6","first":"Alan","key":"roytmanpau","last":"Roytman","locid":"a88f23fb-e4a1-40c8-b349-86e098ab60ea","name":"Roytman, Alan","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"A.","key":"czumajpedh","last":"Czumaj","name":"Czumaj, A.","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1137/1.9781611975031.30","eisbn":["9781611975031"],"pages":"441-456","publisher":"Society for Industrial and Applied Mathematics","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the Twenty-Ninth Annual ACM-SIAM Symposium on Discrete Algorithms ."},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85045560148"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-54394c71-62bd-423e-9e53-a3e2f259317d","ku-c4d1ffa0-a86c-497e-bb0b-e96d63653f99"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The bane of low-dimensionality clustering","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-096.22-034489","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"String attractors [STOC 2018] are combinatorial objects recently introduced to unify all known dictionary compression techniques in a single theory. A set γ ⊆ [1.n] is a k-attractor for a string S ∈ Σn if and only if every distinct substring of S of length at most k has an occurrence crossing at least one of the positions in γ. Finding the smallest k-attractor is NP-hard for k ≥ 3, but polylogarithmic approximations can be found using reductions from dictionary compressors. It is easy to reduce the k-attractor problem to a set-cover instance where the string's positions are interpreted as sets of substrings. The main result of this paper is a much more powerful reduction based on the truncated suffix tree. Our new characterization of the problem leads to more efficient algorithms for string attractors: we show how to check the validity and minimality of a k-attractor in near-optimal time and how to quickly compute exact solutions. For example, we prove that a minimum 3-attractor can be found in O(n) time when |Σ| ∈ O(3+ϵ√log n) for some constant ϵ > 0, despite the problem being NP-hard for large Σ.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Kempa, Dominik","Policriti, Alberto","Prezza, Nicola","Rotenberg, Eva"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-380740"],"event":{"acronym":"ESA","end":"2018-08-22","place":"Helsinki, fi","start":"2018-08-20","title":"26th Annual European Symposium on Algorithms","year":"2018"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Finland","Italy"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Helsinki","University of Pisa","University of Udine"],"keyword":["Set cover"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"string attractors verification and optimization","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.ESA.2018.52","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-380740","identifier":[{"id":"85052501341","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-fe814d21-eb3f-4f31-80c1-196e380cc0c7"],"keyword":["Dictionary compression","Set cover","String attractors"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-F3489140740B0DC28710B59C3C1C97FE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/154948201/LIPIcs_ESA_2018_52.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.ESA.2018.52","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"fi","key":"university of helsinki","loc":["Finland","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Helsinki","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"it","key":"university of udine","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Udine","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"it","key":"university of pisa","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Pisa","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science, Algorithms and Logic"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Dominik","key":"kempapau","last":"Kempa","name":"Kempa, Dominik","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Alberto","key":"policritipau","last":"Policriti","name":"Policriti, Alberto","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Nicola","key":"prezzapau","last":"Prezza","name":"Prezza, Nicola","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Eva","key":"rotenbergpau","last":"Rotenberg","locid":"1a970eb9-57c6-4f5f-a453-117cbc7705ba","name":"Rotenberg, Eva","orcid":"0000-0001-5853-7909","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.4230/lipics.esa.2018.52","isbn":["9783959770811"],"publisher":"Schloss Dagstuhl- Leibniz-Zentrum fur Informatik GmbH, Dagstuhl Publishing","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of 26th European Symposium on Algorithms","volume":"112"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85052501341"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-fe814d21-eb3f-4f31-80c1-196e380cc0c7"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"String attractors : Verification and optimization","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-380740","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Given a graph, does there exist an orientation of the edges such that the resulting directed graph is strongly connected? Robbins' theorem [Robbins, Am. Math. Monthly, 1939] asserts that such an orientation exists if and only if the graph is 2-edge connected. A natural extension of this problem is the following: Suppose that the edges of the graph are partitioned into trails. Can the trails be oriented consistently such that the resulting directed graph is strongly connected? We show that 2-edge connectivity is again a su cient condition and we provide a linear time algorithm for finding such an orientation. The generalised Robbins' theorem [Boesch, Am. Math. Monthly, 1980] for mixed multigraphs asserts that the undirected edges of a mixed multigraph can be oriented to make the resulting directed graph strongly connected exactly when the mixed graph is strongly connected and the underlying graph is bridgeless. We consider the natural extension where the undirected edges of a mixed multigraph are partitioned into trails. It turns out that in this case the condition of the generalised Robbin's Theorem is not su cient. However, we show that as long as each cut either contains at least 2 undirected edges or directed edges in both directions, there exists an orientation of the trails such that the resulting directed graph is strongly connected. Moreover, if the condition is satisfied, we may start by orienting an arbitrary trail in an arbitrary direction. Using this result one obtains a very simple polynomial time algorithm for finding a strong trail orientation if it exists, both in the undirected and the mixed setting.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Aamand, Anders","Hjuler, Niklas","Holm, Jacob","Rotenberg, Eva"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-443052"],"event":{"acronym":"ICALP","end":"2018-07-13","place":"Prague, cz","start":"2018-07-09","title":"45th International Colloquium on Automata, Languages, and Programming, ICALP 2018","year":"2018"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Graph algorithms"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"one wasy trail orientation","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.ICALP.2018.6","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-443052","identifier":[{"id":"85049793618","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-531e4349-16bb-4b42-b7a5-844e6cc665c8"],"keyword":["Graph algorithms","Graph orientation","Robbins' theorem"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-A9B2179C59F6F417547F4455D079B8C2"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/236510539/OA_One_wasy_trail_orientation.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.ICALP.2018.6","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Algorithms and Complexity"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Anders","key":"aamandpau","last":"Aamand","locid":"9a5990a0-c966-4fe5-97d0-a65ea981a0a7","name":"Aamand, Anders","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Niklas","key":"hjulerpau","last":"Hjuler","locid":"7c4e4dd2-ffc7-46b8-93ff-bcd75a916bb4","name":"Hjuler, Niklas","orcid":"0000-0002-0815-670X","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jacob","key":"holmpau","last":"Holm","locid":"b9128f70-a478-4df0-9f6b-cc665f65976c","name":"Holm, Jacob","orcid":"0000-0001-6997-9251","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Eva","key":"rotenbergpau","last":"Rotenberg","name":"Rotenberg, Eva","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Christos","key":"kaklamanispedh","last":"Kaklamanis","name":"Kaklamanis, Christos","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Daniel","key":"marxpedh","last":"Marx","name":"Marx, Daniel","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Ioannis","key":"chatzigiannakispedh","last":"Chatzigiannakis","name":"Chatzigiannakis, Ioannis","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Donald","key":"sannellapedh","last":"Sannella","name":"Sannella, Donald","rank":8,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.4230/lipics.icalp.2018.6","eisbn":["9783959770767"],"issn":["18688969"],"publisher":"Schloss Dagstuhl-Leibniz-Zentrum fuer Informatik","publisher_org":"Schloss Dagstuhl--Leibniz-Zentrum fuer Informatik","series":"Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics, LIPIcs","status":"p","title":"45th International Colloquium on Automata, Languages, and Programming, ICALP 2018"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85049793618"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-531e4349-16bb-4b42-b7a5-844e6cc665c8"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"One-wasy trail orientation","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-443052","year":"2018","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"We consider the communication complexity of fundamental longest common prefix $$({{\\mathrm{\\textsc {Lcp}}}})$$ problems. In the simplest version, two parties, Alice and Bob, each hold a string, A and B, and we want to determine the length of their longest common prefix $$\\ell ={{\\mathrm{\\textsc {Lcp}}}}(A,B)$$ using as few rounds and bits of communication as possible. We show that if the longest common prefix of A and B is compressible, then we can significantly reduce the number of rounds compared to the optimal uncompressed protocol, while achieving the same (or fewer) bits of communication. Namely, if the longest common prefix has an LZ77 parse of z phrases, only $$O(\\lg z)$$ rounds and $$O(\\lg \\ell )$$ total communication is necessary. We extend the result to the natural case when Bob holds a set of strings $$B_1, \\ldots , B_k$$ , and the goal is to find the length of the maximal longest prefix shared by A and any of $$B_1, \\ldots , B_k$$ . Here, we give a protocol with $$O(\\log z)$$ rounds and $$O(\\lg z \\lg k + \\lg \\ell )$$ total communication. We present our result in the public-coin model of computation but by a standard technique our results generalize to the private-coin model. Furthermore, if we view the input strings as integers the problems are the greater-than problem and the predecessor problem.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Bille, Philip","Ettienne, Mikko Berggren","Grossi, Roberto","Gørtz, Inge Li","Rotenberg, Eva"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-357378"],"event":{"end":"2018-10-11","place":"Lima, pe","start":"2018-10-09","title":"25th International Symposium on String Processing and Information Retrieval","year":"2018"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Italy"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Pisa"],"keyword":["Communication complexity","LZ77","Longest common prefix"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"compressed communication complexity of longest common prefixes","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/190555009/1806.03102.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-357378","identifier":[{"id":"85054807721","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-b0d17039-39a1-471b-88d9-24a5c3ab75f6"],"keyword":["Communication complexity","Compression Upper bound","LZ77","Longest common prefix","Output sensitive","Predecessor"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-AB5EFCE08FD3FE7BB32E643838CCD323"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/190555009/1806.03102.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science, Algorithms and Logic"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"it","key":"university of pisa","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Pisa","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Philip","key":"billepau","last":"Bille","locid":"81ad6edf-aa11-47bc-adaf-227fe7e6963e","name":"Bille, Philip","orcid":"0000-0002-1120-5154","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mikko","key":"berggreenettiennepau","last":"Berggreen Ettienne","locid":"fb626c4a-1971-4573-8793-8d4355f314b6","name":"Ettienne, Mikko Berggren","name_org":"Berggreen Ettienne, Mikko","orcid":"0000-0002-8491-2116","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Roberto","key":"grossipau","last":"Grossi","name":"Grossi, Roberto","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Inge Li","key":"gørtzpau","last":"Gørtz","locid":"08da98e0-b1f4-492e-94e7-15083cc40701","name":"Gørtz, Inge Li","orcid":"0000-0002-8322-4952","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Eva","key":"rotenbergpau","last":"Rotenberg","locid":"1a970eb9-57c6-4f5f-a453-117cbc7705ba","name":"Rotenberg, Eva","orcid":"0000-0001-5853-7909","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-030-00479-8_7","isbn":["9783030004798"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"74-87","publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","title":"String Processing and Information Retrieval"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85054807721"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-b0d17039-39a1-471b-88d9-24a5c3ab75f6"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Compressed Communication Complexity of Longest Common Prefixes","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-357378","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"We present a deterministic fully-dynamic data structure for maintaining information about the bridges in a graph. We support updates in Õ((log n)2) amortized time, and can find a bridge in the component of any given vertex, or a bridge separating any two given vertices, in O(log n/ log log n) worst case time. Our bounds match the current best for bounds for deterministic fully-dynamic connectivity up to log log n factors. The previous best dynamic bridge finding was an Õ((log n)3) amortized time algorithm by Thorup [STOC2000], which was a bittrick-based improvement on the O((log n)4) amortized time algorithm by Holm et al.[STOC98, JACM2001]. Our approach is based on a different and purely combinatorial improvement of the algorithim of Holm et al., which by itself gives a new combinatorial Õ((log n)3) amortized time algorithm. Combining it with Thorup's bittrick, we get down to the claimed Õ((log n)2) amortized time. Essentially the same new trick can be applied to the biconnectivity data structure from [STOC98, JACM2001], improving the amortized update time to Õ((log n)3). We also offer improvements in space. We describe a general trick which applies to both of our new algorithms, and to the old ones, to get down to linear space, where the previous best use O(m + n log n log log n). Our result yields an improved running time for deciding whether a unique perfect matching exists in a static graph.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Holm, Jacob","Rotenberg, Eva","Thorup, Mikkel"],"cuid":["S03-090.06-032906"],"event":{"acronym":"SODA","end":"2018-01-10","place":"New Orleans, us","start":"2018-01-07","title":"29th Annual ACM-SIAM Symposium on Discrete Algorithms","year":"2018"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"dynamic bridge finding in i õ i log sup 000000000002 sup i n i amortized time","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/1.9781611975031.3","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S03-090.06-032906","identifier":[{"id":"85045575553","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-0e48e5ff-4671-4134-8ac7-a1a60581d1e5","dtu-ce80d95e-7db9-43d6-908a-129bdeee3ca2","ku-c49ca067-ffab-47b6-8139-b4c192de1c3d"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-E58AC502B9DD589E711BA463E0C21462"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/202169288/1.9781611975031.3.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/1.9781611975031.3","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/142465959/1707.06311.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Algorithms and Programming Languages (APL)","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science, Algorithms and Logic"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Jacob","key":"holmpau","last":"Holm","locid":"b9128f70-a478-4df0-9f6b-cc665f65976c","name":"Holm, Jacob","orcid":"0000-0001-6997-9251","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Eva","key":"rotenbergpau","last":"Rotenberg","locid":"1a970eb9-57c6-4f5f-a453-117cbc7705ba","name":"Rotenberg, Eva","orcid":"0000-0001-5853-7909","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Mikkel","key":"thoruppau","last":"Thorup","locid":"9a753f32-9b71-4058-8b1e-a94b71903f10","name":"Thorup, Mikkel","orcid":"0000-0001-5237-1709","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Artur","key":"czumajpedh","last":"Czumaj","name":"Czumaj, Artur","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1137/1.9781611975031.3","eisbn":["9781611975031"],"pages":"35-52","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","series":"Proceedings of the Annual ACM-SIAM Symposium on Discrete Algorithms","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the Twenty-Ninth Annual ACM-SIAM Symposium on Discrete Algorithms"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85045575553"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-0e48e5ff-4671-4134-8ac7-a1a60581d1e5","dtu-ce80d95e-7db9-43d6-908a-129bdeee3ca2","ku-c49ca067-ffab-47b6-8139-b4c192de1c3d"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Dynamic bridge-finding in <i>õ</i>(log<sup>2</sup> <i>n</i>) amortized time","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-090.06-032906","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"In the online bipartite matching problem with replacements, all the vertices on one side of the bipartition are given, and the vertices on the other side arrive one by one with all their incident edges. The goal is to maintain a maximum matching while minimizing the number of changes (replacements) to the matching. We show that the greedy algorithm that always takes the shortest augmenting path from the newly inserted vertex (denoted the SAP protocol) uses at most amortized O(log2 n) replacements per insertion, where n is the total number of vertices inserted. This is the first analysis to achieve a polylogarithmic number of replacements for any replacement strategy, almost matching the Ω(log n) lower bound. The previous best strategy known achieved amortized O(√n) replacements [Bosek, Leniowski, Sankowski, Zych, FOCS 2014]. For the SAP protocol in particular, nothing better than then trivial O(n) bound was known except in special cases. Our analysis immediately implies the same upper bound of O(log2 n) reassignments for the capacitated assignment problem, where each vertex on the static side of the bipartition is initialized with the capacity to serve a number of vertices. We also analyze the problem of minimizing the maximum server load. We show that if the final graph has maximum server load L, then the SAP protocol makes amortized O(min{L log2 n, √n log n})  reassignments. We also show that this is close to tight because Ω(min{L,√n}) reassignments can be necessary.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Bernstein, Aaron","Holm, Jacob","Rotenberg, Eva"],"cuid":["S02-085.00-037034"],"event":{"acronym":"SODA","end":"2018-01-10","place":"New Orleans, us","start":"2018-01-07","title":"29th Annual ACM-SIAM Symposium on Discrete Algorithms","year":"2018"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Technical University of Berlin"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"online bipartite matching with amortized i o i log sup 000000000002 sup i n i replacements","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85045564975","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-085.00-037034","identifier":[{"id":"85045564975","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-c45275e4-ee4f-43d7-a474-ea56d8f482ca","ku-366f19df-880e-4832-950e-7a6b0a9c992e"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-DF1BFFBA07EE07E298566996404B077C"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"technical university of berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Technical University of Berlin","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Algorithms and Complexity"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science, Algorithms and Logic"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Aaron","key":"bernsteinpau","last":"Bernstein","name":"Bernstein, Aaron","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Jacob","key":"holmpau","last":"Holm","locid":"b9128f70-a478-4df0-9f6b-cc665f65976c","name":"Holm, Jacob","orcid":"0000-0001-6997-9251","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Eva","key":"rotenbergpau","last":"Rotenberg","locid":"1a970eb9-57c6-4f5f-a453-117cbc7705ba","name":"Rotenberg, Eva","orcid":"0000-0001-5853-7909","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Artur","key":"czumajpedh","last":"Czumaj","name":"Czumaj, Artur","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1137/1.9781611975031.61","eisbn":["9781611975031"],"pages":"947-959","publisher":"Society for Industrial and Applied Mathematics","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the Twenty-Ninth Annual ACM-SIAM Symposium on Discrete Algorithms"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85045564975"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-c45275e4-ee4f-43d7-a474-ea56d8f482ca","ku-366f19df-880e-4832-950e-7a6b0a9c992e"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Online bipartite matching with amortized <i>O</i>(log<sup>2</sup> <i>n</i>) replacements","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-085.00-037034","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"We present an algorithm to support the dynamic embedding in the plane of a dynamic graph. An edge can be inserted across a face between two vertices on the face boundary (we call such a vertex pair linkable), and edges can be deleted. The planar embedding can also be changed locally by flipping components that are connected to the rest of the graph by at most two vertices. Given vertices u,v, linkable(u,v) decides whether u and v are linkable in the current embedding, and if so, returns a list of suggestions for the placement of (u,v) in the embedding. For non-linkable vertices u,v, we define a new query, one-flip- linkable(u,v) providing a suggestion for a flip that will make them linkable if one exists. We support all updates and queries in O(log 2 n) time. Our time bounds match those of Italiano et al. for a static (flipless) embedding of a dynamic graph. Our new algorithm is simpler, exploiting that the complement of a spanning tree of a connected plane graph is a spanning tree of the dual graph. The primal and dual trees are interpreted as having the same Euler tour, and a main idea of the new algorithm is an elegant interaction between top trees over the two trees via their common Euler tour.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Holm, Jacob","Rotenberg, Eva"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-412956"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Data structures","Dynamic data structures","Graph algorithms","Graph embeddings","Graph theory","Planar graphs"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"dynamic planar embeddings of dynamic graphs","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","type":"rem","url":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/1704.00565","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-412956","identifier":[{"id":"85018508772","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-20be1c54-ee46-4957-a0cd-3edbb9890877"],"keyword":["Data structures","Dynamic data structures","Graph algorithms","Graph embeddings","Graph theory","Planar graphs"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-56928CEA246DF6C3878254F53AAF3EBC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","type":"rem","url":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/1704.00565","version":"Pre-print"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2017","yearSub":"2017"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Jacob","key":"holmpau","last":"Holm","locid":"b9128f70-a478-4df0-9f6b-cc665f65976c","name":"Holm, Jacob","orcid":"0000-0001-6997-9251","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Eva","key":"rotenbergpau","last":"Rotenberg","locid":"00219c09-8417-495f-96ff-eb34884ad59c","name":"Rotenberg, Eva","orcid":"0000-0001-5853-7909","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s00224-017-9768-7","issn":["14324350","14330490"],"issn_org":["14324350"],"issue":"4","journal":"Theory of Computing Systems","pages":"1054-1083","status":"p","title":"Theory of Computing Systems","volume":"61"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85018508772"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-20be1c54-ee46-4957-a0cd-3edbb9890877"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Dynamic planar embeddings of dynamic graphs","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-412956","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"A graph consists of a set of vertices and a set of edges between vertices. Graphs are a popular mathematical model for road maps, communication networks, electrical circuits, social networks, disease transmission networks, job assignments, resource allocation, and more. A special class of graphs are planar graphs, which are those that can be drawn on a piece of paper without any pair of edges crossing. For planar graphs where each edge can only be traversed in one direction, a fundamental question is whether there is a route from vertex A to vertex B in the graph. We show how such a graph can be represented in a very compact way (linear space), such that we can still answer very fast (constant time) to questions of the form: \"is there a path from A to B?\" When edges can be traversed in either direction, the graph is connected if for any two vertices of the graph, there is a path between them. However, for many practical purposes, e.g. in communication networks, it is important for the graph to be highly connected: If the removal of few vertices or edges would disconnect the graph, this points to a vulnerability in the network; an indication that the network connectivity may be about to break down. This is particularly challenging, because in the real world, graphs rarely stay as they are: Links in the communication network fail, a road becomes inaccessible or slow, or — social networks change all the time. We show how to represent a graph in a compact way, such that we can give fast answers to questions of the form: \"Is there an edge such that all paths between A and B go via that edge?\" and which can quickly be updated when edges are inserted or deleted. We further show how to represent a planar graph such that we can quickly update our representation when an edge is deleted, and such that questions of the form: \"How many non-crossing paths are there between A and B?\" can quickly (in constant time) be answered by: \"None\", \"One\", \"Two\", or \"At least 3\". Higher connectivity also has theoretical applications. Indeed, we study the problem of finding a tour that visits every vertex exactly once, and such that two consecutive vertices are at most 2 edges apart. For general graphs, it is NP-hard even to decide if such a tour exists, but when the graph is highly connected in the sense that it does not contain a vertex whose removal would disconnect it, we give a linear time algorithm for outputting such a tour. The previous best algorithm for this had quadratic running time. Finally, we study online bipartite graphs. Imagine a number of servers are given in advance, and then, clients arrive one by one. Each client can only be served by a subset of the servers, and each server can only serve a fixed number of clients. We want to at all times serve as many clients at possible, but then, we may sometimes need to reassign a client to a different server. We show that very few reassignments are necessary. Assessment Committee","author_count":1,"contributors":["Rotenberg, Eva"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-472188"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"algorithms and data structures for graphs","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"do","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://soeg.kb.dk/permalink/45KBDK_KGL/fbp0ps/alma99122798881605763","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-472188","ids":["ku-8395eb94-095e-4afa-a69c-84e388a739fe"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-E6B39FF79EC677EA6A3F69C2B03FDBA9"],"org":[{"affno":1,"key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, SCIENCE Faculty Office, SCIENCE PhD theses"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU"}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Eva","key":"rotenbergpau","last":"Rotenberg","locid":"00219c09-8417-495f-96ff-eb34884ad59c","name":"Rotenberg, Eva","orcid":"0000-0001-5853-7909","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"publisher":"Department of Computer Science, Faculty of Science, University of Copenhagen","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-8395eb94-095e-4afa-a69c-84e388a739fe"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Algorithms and Data Structures for Graphs","type":"Other","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-472188","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"We answer the following question dating back to J. E. Littlewood (1885-1977): Can two lions catch a man in a bounded area with rectifiable lakes? The lions and the man are all assumed to be points moving with at most unit speed. That the lakes are rectifiable means that their boundaries are finitely long. This requirement is to avoid pathological examples where the man survives forever because any path to the lions is infinitely long. We show that the answer to the question is not always \"yes\" by giving an example of a region R in the plane where the man has a strategy to survive forever. R is a polygonal region with holes and the exterior and interior boundaries are pairwise disjoint, simple polygons. Our construction is the first truly two-dimensional example where the man can survive. Next, we consider the following game played on the entire plane instead of a bounded area: There is any finite number of unit speed lions and one fast man who can run with speed 1 + ϵ for some value ϵ > 0. Can the man always survive? We answer the question in the affirmative for any constant ϵ > 0.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Abrahamsen, Mikkel","Holm, Jacob","Rotenberg, Eva","Wulff-Nilsen, Christian"],"cuid":["S02-088.70-036971"],"event":{"acronym":"SCG","end":"2017-07-07","place":"Brisbane, au","start":"2017-07-04","title":"33rd International Symposium on Computational Geometry, SoCG 2017","year":"2017"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Lion and man game","Pursuit evasion game","Winning strategy"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"best laid plans of lions and men","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.SoCG.2017.6","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-088.70-036971","identifier":[{"id":"85029940351","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-c16e63da-d6e4-4695-b2f7-7a44c7a521a7","ku-463cd80b-acb3-472f-86d0-8157e0cbc0d2"],"keyword":["Lion and man game","Pursuit evasion game","Winning strategy"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-3437B036E3A64F8B8D1E829FF0C92EB5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/188448938/Abrahamsen_2017_Best_laid_plans.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.SoCG.2017.6","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2017","yearSub":"2017"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Algorithms and Programming Languages (APL)","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science, Algorithms and Logic"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Mikkel","key":"abrahamsenpau","last":"Abrahamsen","locid":"63596832-461f-409a-a2cc-81023b9b8e46","name":"Abrahamsen, Mikkel","orcid":"0000-0003-2734-4690","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jacob","key":"holmpau","last":"Holm","locid":"b9128f70-a478-4df0-9f6b-cc665f65976c","name":"Holm, Jacob","orcid":"0000-0001-6997-9251","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Eva","key":"rotenbergpau","last":"Rotenberg","locid":"1a970eb9-57c6-4f5f-a453-117cbc7705ba","name":"Rotenberg, Eva","orcid":"0000-0001-5853-7909","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Christian","key":"wulffnilsenpau","last":"Wulff-Nilsen","locid":"10a26f7c-95c5-4716-ba80-32ad40ba4f07","name":"Wulff-Nilsen, Christian","orcid":"0000-0002-3699-7821","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Boris","key":"aronovpedh","last":"Aronov","name":"Aronov, Boris","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Matthew J.","key":"katzpedh","last":"Katz","name":"Katz, Matthew J.","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.4230/lipics.socg.2017.6","eisbn":["9783959770385"],"issn":["18688969"],"publisher":"Schloss Dagstuhl - Leibniz-Zentrum für Informatik","series":"Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics","status":"p","title":"33rd International Symposium on Computational Geometry (SoCG 2017)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85029940351"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-c16e63da-d6e4-4695-b2f7-7a44c7a521a7","ku-463cd80b-acb3-472f-86d0-8157e0cbc0d2"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Best laid plans of lions and men","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-088.70-036971","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"We present a data structure that can maintain a simple planar graph under edge contractions in linear total time. The data structure supports adjacency queries and provides access to neighbor lists in O(1) time. Moreover, it can report all the arising self-loops and parallel edges. By applying the data structure, we can achieve optimal running times for decremental bridge detection, 2-edge connectivity, maximal 3-edge connected components, and the problem of finding a unique perfect matching for a static planar graph. Furthermore, we improve the running times of algorithms for several planar graph problems, including decremental 2-vertex and 3- edge connectivity, and we show that using our data structure in a black-box manner, one obtains conceptually simple optimal algorithms for computing MST and 5-coloring in planar graphs.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Holm, Jacob","Italiano, Giuseppe F.","Karczmarz, Adam","Łacki, Jakub","Rotenberg, Eva","Sankowski, Piotr"],"cuid":["S02-089.58-036713"],"event":{"acronym":"ESA","end":"2017-09-06","place":"Vienna, at","start":"2017-09-04","title":"25th European Symposium on Algorithms (ESA 2017)","year":"2017"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Italy","Poland","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Alphabet Inc.","Google","University of Rome Tor Vergata","University of Warsaw"],"keyword":["Algorithms","Coloring","Connectivity","Data structures","Planar graphs"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"contracting a planar graph efficiently","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","licen